 Vaishali Patel, Senior Manager - Research- Technical Department at Jainam 
Aegis Vopak Terminals is showing signs of renewed strength after breaking above a declining trendline that had capped its upside since the July peak. The stock has been forming a series of higher lows, indicating steady accumulation at lower levels. Despite marginal profit booking on Wednesday, the price continues to trade above its key short- and medium-term moving averages, keeping the overall trend positive.
The breakout was accompanied by improved volumes, suggesting genuine buying interest rather than a temporary bounce. Momentum indicators remain supportive, with the RSI sustaining above the 60 mark, reflecting improving bullish traction. The stock has also been outperforming the broader market, as evidenced by the rising relative strength line.
ACME Solar Holdings has witnessed a strong breakout above the crucial Rs 400–405 resistance zone, signalling a continuation of its ongoing uptrend. The stock continues to trade above its key moving averages, reflecting a robust bullish structure and sustained buying interest. The breakout is backed by healthy volumes, indicating strong market participation and accumulation at higher levels.
Momentum remains favourable, with the RSI moving above 60, suggesting strengthening bullish momentum. A sustained move above Rs 420 could pave the way for further upside towards Rs 440–460 in the near term. Meanwhile, the Rs 400–405 zone is expected to act as strong support during any corrective decline. Overall, the technical setup remains constructive, with a positive bias in the short-to-medium term.
 Sudeep Shah, Head - Technical and Derivatives Research at SBI Securities 
 Prestige Estates Projects | CMP: Rs 1,424 
Prestige Estates Projects had been consolidating in the 1,670–1,522 range throughout August before breaking below the lower end of the range and witnessing a sharp decline. The stock has slipped below its key moving averages, indicating deterioration in the near-term price structure and sustained selling pressure. The RSI is in a falling mode, reflecting weakening momentum and a bearish bias.
Additionally, the rising red histogram bars on the MACD indicator further reinforce the negative setup. The ADX has also started to rise, suggesting that the prevailing bearish trend is gaining strength. Sustenance below the breakdown zone could pave the way for further downside, while the overall bias remains bearish.
Hence, selling is recommended in the Rs 1,435–1,420 zone, with a stop-loss of Rs 1,470. On the downside, the stock is likely to test Rs 1,340 in the short term.
PB Fintech witnessed a downward-sloping trendline breakout four weeks ago and moved higher before undergoing a throwback towards the earlier breakout zone. This zone subsequently acted as a strong support base, indicating buying interest at lower levels. The stock has now reclaimed its 20-day and 50-day EMAs, pointing towards an improvement in near-term sentiment.
The RSI has also risen from 44 to 56, signalling renewed bullish momentum. On the weekly chart, the RSI has rebounded from around the 60 level, keeping the broader bullish bias intact. Sustenance above the breakout zone could support further upside, while the stock's near-term bias remains positive as long as it holds above the key moving averages.
Hence, accumulation is recommended in the Rs 1,820–1,840 zone, with a stop-loss of Rs 1,760. On the upside, the stock is likely to test Rs 1,960 in the short term.
 Allied Blenders and Distillers | CMP: Rs 647.55 
