The US Federal Reserve on September 16 hiked fed funds rate by 25 basis points, and markets have already priced in another 25 basis point rate hike. That is not a surprise. What the market likes is that the Fed is maintaining its credential as an independent institution. Ten-year US Treasury yields sitting above 5 percent are the bond market’s way of saying the same thing: inflation has stayed above target for too long, and fiscal arithmetic is no longer a footnote.
The United States earns about $5.5 trillion and spends about $7.5 trillion. A $2 trillion annual deficit on a $40 trillion debt stock is not a one-year problem. It is a trajectory. When debt is rising faster than income, two things can happen. If the dollar depreciates more than the extra yield the Fed is offering, money can leave. If the dollar firms along with higher rates, money can still flow in. Direction of the currency will decide the direction of flows more than the size of the hike.
There is another layer. A large part of the current US growth narrative is being financed by debt rather than free cash flow, especially in AI. Token costs in the US are high. If China keeps pushing cheaper, open-source models and users confirm they work well enough, US LLM economics come under pressure. That is not a prediction of collapse. It is a reminder that bubbles funded by leverage rather than cash flow do not last forever.
For India the immediate variables are different. A monsoon deficit of around 15 percent, oil in triple-digit, supply-chain disruption, and higher US yields form a difficult cocktail. Add FPI selling and a heavy IPO calendar, and the market feels the pressure even when corporate earnings have been solid. Valuations today are fair—neither cheap nor expensive. That is not a call to abandon equities. It is a call to stop treating 20–30 percent returns as a birthright.
Oil prices and supply-chain disruption matter more for the multiple on the global side than the last 25 basis points on US Fed rate. On the local side, the IPO pipeline is the more immediate supply of paper. Neither is a reason to change the basic discipline.
Investors should stay with their asset allocation. If you are overweight equity, this is not the moment to add more. If you are underweight, use weakness in a calibrated way. If you are equal-weight, continue SIPs and do not try to time every headline. Diversify across equity, debt, and precious metals. Within equity, prefer businesses that can absorb higher input costs and still grow earnings.
Markets do not move in a straight line. Last year’s returns will not automatically repeat. What repeats is the need to buy good businesses run by good managers at reasonable prices, and to keep the portfolio balanced. Follow the dharma of asset allocation. The rest is noise.
