A Rs 1 crore home may look straightforward on paper: arrange the down payment, take a home loan for the rest and start paying the EMI. In practice, the amount you need before getting the keys can be considerably higher than the down payment alone.
For a home costing more than Rs 75 lakh, the Reserve Bank of India’s loan-to-value, or LTV, framework allows banks to finance up to 75 percent of the property value for individual housing loans. On a Rs 1 crore property, that means a loan of up to Rs 75 lakh and at least Rs 25 lakh from your own funds.
But “up to” is doing a lot of work here. A bank is not required to lend the full Rs 75 lakh simply because the property is worth Rs 1 crore. Your income, existing EMIs, credit history, age, employment and the lender’s own assessment can result in a lower sanctioned amount.
That is why it is risky to save exactly Rs 25 lakh and assume you are ready to buy.
There are other upfront expenses. Stamp duty and registration charges vary across states and, in many cases, add a substantial amount to the purchase cost. These charges are generally not included in the property value for calculating the bank’s LTV. So, the buyer usually needs to arrange them separately.
Suppose you are buying a Rs 1 crore flat and the bank sanctions Rs 75 lakh. Your basic contribution towards the property would be Rs 25 lakh. Now add stamp duty, registration and other transaction-related expenses. Depending on the state, property location and applicable charges, the cash requirement can move well beyond Rs 25 lakh.
There is another calculation that prospective buyers sometimes overlook: the EMI.
A Rs 75 lakh home loan over 20 years can mean a sizeable monthly commitment. The exact EMI will depend on the interest rate offered by the lender. A longer tenure can bring down the monthly payment, but it also means paying interest for more years.
For someone with a monthly take-home income of Rs 1.5 lakh, for example, a large home-loan EMI could leave considerably less room for school fees, insurance, investments, household expenses and emergencies. The bank may approve the loan, but that does not automatically mean the loan is comfortable for the family budget.
This is why the right down payment is not necessarily the smallest amount the bank permits. Putting more money upfront reduces the loan and, consequently, the interest burden. But draining your savings to increase the down payment can create another problem.
A home purchase should not leave you with almost no cash. Keep a separate emergency fund and account for near-term expenses such as interiors, shifting, repairs, furniture or society-related charges. If the property is under construction, there may also be additional costs during the purchase and possession process.
There is also a temptation to borrow the down payment through a personal loan or credit card. That can make an already large housing commitment more expensive. Personal loans generally carry higher interest rates than home loans, while revolving credit card debt can be particularly costly.
A sensible approach is to work backwards. Start with the home price, calculate the likely loan amount and EMI, add government and transaction charges, and then see how much cash you can put in without wiping out your savings.
For a Rs 1 crore home, Rs 25 lakh may be the minimum contribution implied by the 75 percent LTV ceiling. But the financially safer target is the amount that covers the purchase costs while still leaving you with enough money to handle life after the purchase.
